{"questions":[{"question_id":"mR02r09kEHwQ2JAA","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2007","year":2007,"examDate":2007,"subject":"physics","chapter":"magnetics","chapterGroup":"electricity","topic":"motion-of-charged-particle-inside-magnetic-field","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"a-charged-particle-moves-through-a-magnetic-field-perpendicu-2007-marks-4-mr02r09kehwq2jaa.htm","content":"A charged particle moves through a magnetic field perpendicular to its direction. Then ","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Kinetic energy changes but the momentum is constant "},{"identifier":"B","content":"the momentum changes but the kinetic energy is constant "},{"identifier":"C","content":"both momentum and kinetic energy of the particle are not constant "},{"identifier":"D","content":"both momentum and kinetic energy of the particle are constant "}],"correct_options":["B"],"explanation":"<b>NOTE :</b> When a charged particle enters a magnetic field at a direction perpendicular to the direction of motion, the path of the motion is circular. In circular motion the direction of velocity changes at every point (the magnitude remains constant).\n<br><br>Therefore,  the tangential momentum will change at every point. But kinetic energy will remain constant as it is given by <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.781ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.245ex\" height=\"2.737ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864.9 2760.1 1209.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink